    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d533">

                <pb facs="0731" n="719" sameAs="frus:frus1946v08/pg719"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">102.2/7–2646: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Political Adviser in Korea</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Langdon</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Seoul</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-07-26">26 July 1946</date>.<lb/>[Received July 26—5:45
                        p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Tfurc 56 [to Tokyo]. Following is a report of developments since our Tfurc 54 of
                    3 July.</p>
                <pb facs="0732" n="720" xml:id="pg720"/>
                <p>Unity talks have progressed to the stage where both Right and Left have formally
                    appointed five delegates each to meet and substantiate unity. Rightist are Dr.
                    Kimm Kiu Sik, Won Sie Hoon,<anchor n="36" xml:id="d533-f1"/> An Chai
                        Hong,<anchor n="37" xml:id="d533-f2"/> Choi Tong Oh and Kim Boon Chun;
                    Leftist are Lyuh Woon Hyung, Hu Hun, Kim Wong Bong, Paik Nam Un and Lee Kang
                    Kook. Choi is Chairman of pool of mainly Rightist parties known as Emergency
                    National Congress. Won is Vice Chairman of Representative Democratic
                    Council.</p>
                <closer>[<signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Langdon</hi></signed>]</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d533-f1" n="36" xml:id="d533-f1-content">
                <p>Won Sai Hoon was regarded as the actual head of the Democratic Party in south
                    Korea.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d533-f2" n="37" xml:id="d533-f2-content">
                <p>An Chai Hong helped to found the Nationalist Party after leaving the People’s
                    Party because of its radicalism.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
